			<div class="content" id="post-content-4259939">
									

<p>this is, an interesting skip that does some interesting things but there's so much going on and it feels like so many really long blocks of text that it ends up being a little boring to read imo; but this might just be that i'm not good at reading lots of information.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;nitpicks</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;nitpicks</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<ul>
<li>&quot;Description: SCP-4730is&quot; is missing a space between &quot;4730&quot; and &quot;is&quot;</li>
<li>it feels a little weird to me that the security camera footage of Incident 4730-A doesn't require level 4 clearance but the video log transcript does? it's probably not a big issue though</li>
<li>the &quot;Notice From Site-70 Nu-7 Battery Captain Pelterie&quot; feels weird; the stuff it suggests reading needs at least level 4 clearance, there's an immediate &quot;by order of the overseer council&quot; after it which makes it feel a little like there should be something between the two to me, and i'm not sure what it really adds here</li>
<li>the overseer council order asks for Level 4 Security Credentials, and the collapsible after says the Level 5 security credentials should be entered and it feels like they should be the same level; and that the collapsible should say something like &quot;input security credentials&quot; before expanding instead of &quot;Level 5 Clearance Accepted&quot;</li>
<li>Incident 4730-A Video Log Transcript uses dd/mm/yyyy; Interrogation 4730-A Video Log Transcript uses mm/dd/yyyy; and the other two are ambiguous (in that the date format could be either way around)</li>
<li>the use of &quot;natives&quot; by the greater foundation to describe people they've kidnapped feels weird but isn't inherently wrong</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
